hi I'm Nick Gillespie for Reason TV  today we're talking with mikela Rong  author of it's our turn to eat the story  of a Kenyan whistleblower who is the  Kenyan whistleblower and what is his  story  the Kenyan whistleblower is John  Githongo who was an old friend of mine  when in 2003 a new president came into  Kenya swearing that he was gonna clean  out the corruption of the Moy era he  appointed this man as his  anti-corruption chief and what happened  was that John discovered a whole new  scandal called Anglo leasing and also  discovered that all his closest  colleagues all his ministers and  government that he worked most closely  with were involved in that so then he  faced this predicament do I do I tell  all or do I keep it to myself and so  that's what the books about  and what did he decide to do and how did  it play out he eventually decided to go  public and he was an extraordinary  position because he had taken this  precaution of taping his conversation  over the years with with these  ministerial colleagues who were saying  to him openly  you know this scandal is us you know  it's it's it's it's your own ethnic  group behind us so he had all this  material which I think is unprecedented  in Africa probably in in most countries  so he compiled the dossier eventually  leaked it to the press and that's  essentially the story and seeing the  fallout because on the one hand there  were a few people who stepped aside who  were asked to leave office but  essentially nobody ended up returning  the money  nobody was prosecuted and one of the  things I try and pull out in my in my  book is that I think the donor community  in Kenya and often in Africa has proved  to be very very lacks and very  complacent about these kind of  corruption scandals that go to the heart  of MIS governance in Africa so in the  wake of everybody acknowledging that  corruption is rampant that it had  survived the Moore regime there was  really no change then there was lip  service paid  in fact I believe that the businessman  who went to the new ministers and said  here's a great way of making money here  all these da G's security and military  contracts were almost certainly people  who had dealt with the previous regime  so there was a very smooth continuance  and I think what happened was that a  rather naive and wishful thinking donor  community which desperately wanted to  start lending money which it had frozen  off undermine it wanted to keep to start  up the money again to the kavacha regime  they really didn't want to see the  evidence of their own eyes what is the  larger significance of this Kenyan story  I mean everybody recognizes that if you  have a corrupt government or a corrupt  regime you really can't do economic  development you can't really have  political freedom or political stability  how does the Kenyan story translate to a  larger story about Africa or about the  developing world generally well I would  disagree with you I don't think everyone  does recognize the things you said they  should but they don't and I think what  we've seen in the last few years in  Africa is the sort of Jeffrey Sachs  school of thought which is about putting  the emphasis on more and more aid to get  countries out of the poverty trap and  sort of downplaying the politics and  behaving as though local politics which  are very poisonous in places like Kenyan  and very ethnic are irrelevant and you  can help a country despite those  politics and I think what the Kenyan  case showed and it's true of many  African countries is you cannot pretend  to help a country if you don't cast a  very critical eye on the politics of the  day and if you have a government that's  busy stealing you know there is no point  continuing to spout the sermon about  helping and aid and rolling out the  World Bank projects and the IMF so I  mean yours squarely against the kind of  post-war development model which is that  you have organizations like the World  Bank or the International Monetary Fund  which just says there is a huge amount  of money we're helping you this is we  call this development and here's a bunch  of money that you could be you know then  government States officials at every  level just spend whatever  where they want I'm not of the dambisa  Moyo school of thought the author of  dead 8 who sort of says it's all rubbish  cut it off five years and that should be  the end of it but I do think that we  massively overemphasize and over play  the role that aids is going to play in  in these African countries future it  isn't as important as we like to think  it can do more harm than good  it certainly did I think in Kenya you  can undermine people like John Githongo  so what's the best thing that the West  can do to help Africa become a richer  freer place I think at the end of the  day it's not going to be in Western  hands and that's something that we need  to recognize and something we haven't  wanted to recognize with all this  emphasis on on upping aid we can save  lives in terms of malarial bednets  treatment for HIV we can prevent people  from starving we cannot make an African  Administration or do the right thing  and most of the key developments in  Africa the most encouraging ones have  come from the private sector in Africa  can you talk a little bit about them and  I think there are several examples are  routinely quoted the spread of the  internet across Africa the the role of  the mobile phone in terms of linking  people up making them more aware the  spread of the private radio station  spreading the word about governance  democracy anti-corruption these are the  really important factors and actually  these are things that have happened  without that much explain the title of  your book it's our turn to eat what does  that mean it's the phrase you hear a lot  in Kenya you also hear it a lot around  Africa and it's basically about one  ethnic community usually a minority  ethnic community saying if we have our  guy in state house in the presidency  it'll be our communities turn to eat so  he'll take care of us you'll make sure  that contracts go to our group he'll  make sure our constituents who gets the  development money he'll make sure that  it's jobs for the boys your two previous  books were in the footsteps of mr. Kurtz  living on the brink of disaster in the  Congo  and I didn't do it for you how the world  betrayed a small African nation and  you're talking about Eritrea there why  are you interested in Africa I started  working there as a Reuters correspondent  and it just slowly I became de facto  expert and it really has been a  professional interest it's not an  emotional interest so you're not going  to buy a coffee plantation I don't have  a family links and I also I sort of  slightly disliked the sentimentalism you  meet people and they say oh Africa gets  into your blood and you kind of think I  find it a fascinating continent I've got  to know very many different countries  you know each one radically different  from each other it's intellectually  intriguing it's incredibly rich and  diverse but I do slightly rebel against  that idea that you you you fall in love  with a place and then in some way that  kind of blinds you to it it's just the  place that I haven't have worked what  you know in in the post-colonial your  Asia for the most part South American  large part so let's even say Australia  and New Zealand have come a long way  they're almost civilized now but Africa  continues to lag behind in terms of  overall gross domestic product things  like that is it I mean ours or you know  indicators life expectancy is going down  in many African countries what I mean is  there any hope for Africa I mean you  have to look at the history the Cold War  only entered in 89 so when we tend to  lecture African countries about you know  why is it taking so long you said I  think the the area in which the west and  the Soviet Union and China were  interfering in incredibly damaging ways  and manipulating the local political  systems and leaders and and with utter  cynicism is only over since 89 I mean  that is so recent and then you have to  think before then you have the colonial  period the nation-state being very fresh  and before that slavery devastating the  continent so I think you know  these are unique combination of factors  and what you're seeing now is this new  generation coming up who are highly  educated very urban  they are the Gyeonggi fungo generation  they're people who look at their  continent in a different way who are at  ease in this cosmopolitan world we all  live in and I think they are going to  change this continent what is your next  project um I'm actually thinking of  writing a novel when John turned up on  my flat and went into hiding at my place  at the start of this whole story I was  actually writing a novel and I would  like to go back and finish it  what's the novel about okay I just hope  it's not about how Africa like herpes is  in your blood all right well very good  thank you  Michela wrong for talking to us today  author most recently of it's our turn to  eat the story of a Kenyan whistleblower  thank you very much  you
 

